-- title:
--   Lemma 5.9 — |𝒥^P_i(uⁱ, ū⁻ⁱ) − J^P_i(uⁱ)| ≤ C₀/√N + (C₀/N)E∫|uⁱ|² (corrected: Σ̄, M^P, p.17 abbreviations, (11))
-- statement:
--   Under the standing hypotheses of §5, for every $P\in\Upsilon([0,T];\mathbb S^n)$ there is $C_0$ such that, whenever agent $i$ deviates to $u^i\in\mathscr U^i_c$ and the others keep $\bar u$,
--
--   $$|\mathcal J^P_i(u^i,\bar u^{-i})-J^P_i(u^i)|\le\frac{C_0}{\sqrt N}+\frac{C_0}N\mathbb E\int_0^T|u^i_t|^2dt,$$
--
--   where $J^P_i(u^i)$ is (24) along the limiting state of (43). Combined with the uniform convexity of $J^P_i$ (Lemma 4.14) this yields the a-priori bound (44).
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: a complete filtered probability space carrying 2N independent d-dimensional Brownian motions Wⁱ, W̄ⁱ and a 1-dimensional W⁰, with 𝓕 their filtration augmented by the null sets (p.1) → every probability space with one standard Brownian motion of dimension 1+2Nd (independent coordinates), 𝓕 its natural filtration without augmentation; reason: independent coordinates are exactly 2N+1 independent Brownian motions of these dimensions; adapted processes of the augmented filtration have adapted modifications, and every quantity is an expectation
--   - encoding: "the solution" of a linear SDE ((2)–(4), (6)–(7), (10)–(13), (20), (42)–(43)) → every strong solution in the sense of Peng1990.SMP.SolvesSDE for the full filtration (statements quantify over solution processes); reason: solutions exist and are a.s. unique under (H1); quantifying over all of them is the paper's claim for each
--   - encoding: observation filtrations σ{yʲ_s, θ_s}, σ{Yⁱ_s, θ_s}, σ{θ_s} (pp.6, 8) → the same σ-algebras augmented by the measurable P-null sets; reason: makes them independent of the version of the a.s.-unique solution processes that generate them, as the paper's augmented 𝓕 does
--   - encoding: controls uⁱ ∈ L²_𝓕([0,T]; ℝᵐ) with E sup_{t≤T}|uⁱ_t|² < ∞, adapted to the observation filtration → IsCtrl: progressively measurable for 𝓕 with E sup_{t≤T}|u_t|² < ∞, and u_t measurable for the observation σ-algebra at every t ∈ [0,T]; reason: the paper's definition of 𝒰^{i,0}_c, 𝒰^{i,0}_d (pp.6, 8) together with "u ∈ L²_𝓕" (p.6)
--   - encoding: L^∞([0,T]; ·) = deterministic uniformly bounded functions (p.5), L²([0,T]; ·) square integrable → Borel functions of time bounded by a constant at every t ∈ [0,T] (L²: Borel and square integrable); reason: p.5's own definition; Borel measurability fixes the representative so coefficient-times-process integrands are progressive
--   - reading: σ̌ is non-degenerate ((H1), p.6) → |σ̌_t| ≥ κ > 0 for every t ∈ [0,T]; reason: σ̌ is scalar; the standard meaning in [19, Thm 7.16], which the paper invokes on p.3 for 𝓕^θ = 𝓕^{W⁰}
--   - reading: x^{0,0} in (12)–(13), never defined (p.7 says only that x⁽ᴺ⁾₀ is approximated by x^{0,0}) → the solution of dx^{0,0} = (A+Ā)x^{0,0}dt + (D+D̄)x^{0,0}dW⁰, x^{0,0}_0 = x; reason: the conditional-mean limit of (6); any 𝓕^θ-adapted choice gives the same 𝓕^{Y^{i,0}}, the only place it enters
--   - reading: (x⁰, u⁰) "some 𝓕^θ-adapted process pair" (p.7) → 𝓕^θ-adapted on [0,T], progressively measurable, with E sup|x⁰|², E sup|u⁰|² < ∞; reason: needed for the cost (16) to be finite; the pair actually used ((38), (59)) satisfies it (p.23)
--   - correction: (11): dYⁱ = (G Xⁱ + H uⁱ + H̄ x⁰ + Ḡ u⁰ + b̃)dt + σ̃ dW̄ⁱ → dYⁱ = (G Xⁱ + H uⁱ + Ḡ x⁰ + H̄ u⁰ + b̃)dt + σ̃ dW̄ⁱ; reason: ill-typed as printed for m ≠ n (H̄ ∈ ℝ^{n×m} times x⁰ ∈ ℝⁿ, e.g. n = 2, m = 1); the pattern of (3), (13), (15)
--   - reading: 𝒰^{i,0}_d / 𝒰ⁱ_d: "uⁱ ∈ 𝒰^{i,0}_d and uⁱ is 𝓕^{Yⁱ}-adapted" (Def. 4.1) → u ∈ 𝒰^{i,0}_d and u is adapted to σ{Y_s, θ_s} for every solution (X, Y) of (10)–(11) under u; reason: Yⁱ is generated by the control itself; all solutions agree a.s.
--   - reading: Υ([0,T]; 𝕊ⁿ) = {P : P_t = P₀ + ∫₀ᵗ Ṗ_s ds} (p.10), regularity of Ṗ not printed → P 𝕊ⁿ-valued on [0,T] with Ṗ Lebesgue integrable (P absolutely continuous); conditions involving Ṗ hold for some such representative Ṗ at every t; reason: the weakest reading of the printed set; Ṗ is determined only a.e.
--   - encoding: "≥ 0" for the (time-dependent) matrices of (PD) and (26) → nonnegative quadratic form ⟨Mv, v⟩ ≥ 0 at every t ∈ [0,T]; reason: Q^P involves Ṗ, symmetric only a.e.; for symmetric matrices this is positive semidefiniteness
--   - reading: solutions Π ∈ C([0,T]; 𝕊ⁿ), Σ ∈ C([0,T]; ℝ^{n×n}) of (34)–(35) (C = φ̇ continuous, p.5), and ρ of (36) → absolutely continuous solutions satisfying the equation for a.e. t ∈ [0,T], with the terminal condition and the positivity condition (𝓡 ≫ 0, resp. 𝓡̃ + 𝓡̃ᵀ ≫ 0) of the display; reason: with merely bounded measurable coefficients a C¹ solution does not exist in general; App. C (p.23) calls Π, Σ, ρ absolutely continuous; for continuous coefficients the two coincide
--   - correction: Σ̄ = ΣB + (D + D̄)ᵀΣF + (1 − α₂)S (p.13), entering (35), (37), (38), (39), (59) → Σ̄ = ΣᵀB + (D + D̄)ᵀΣᵀF + (1 − α₂)S, i.e. Σ̄ᵀ = BᵀΣ + FᵀΣ(D + D̄) + (1 − α₂)Sᵀ; reason: App. C (p.23) substitutes φ = Π(X̄ − x⁰) + Σx⁰ + ρ and (58) into (18), which yields BᵀΣx⁰ + FᵀΣ(D + D̄)x⁰; the printed form agrees iff Σ is symmetric ((56) types Σ as 𝕊ⁿ-valued, Theorem 4.12 as ℝ^{n×n}, and (35) is asymmetric, Remark 4.13(ii)); with the printed form the pair (38)/(59) is not consistent in general
--   - reading: ūⁱ "given by (18)" (Theorem 5.11; §5 opening: ūⁱ = ū^{i,P} of (32), (x⁰, u⁰) from the consistency condition (31)) → IsDecProfile: every ūⁱ is an optimal control of Problem (MFD) for agent i with an 𝓕^θ-adapted square-integrable (x⁰, u⁰) satisfying (31): x⁰_t = E[X̄ⁱ_t | 𝓕^θ_t] for every t, u⁰_t = E[ūⁱ_t | 𝓕^θ_t] for a.e. t; reason: the defining properties of the decentralized strategies; Proposition 4.11 and Theorem 4.12 give existence, and the feedback form (37) is Theorem 4.12's conclusion, not the definition
--   - reading: O(1/N), O(1/√N) with "C₀ a constant independent of N" (p.15) → ∃ C₀ such that the bound C₀/N (resp. C₀/√N) holds for every N ≥ 1, uniformly in the agent and the probability space; reason: the printed meaning of the constant; every quantity is finite for each N
--   - encoding: E[sup_{0≤t≤T}|·|²], E∫₀ᵀ|·|²dt → supSq / intSq: lower Lebesgue integrals in [0, ∞] of the squared Euclidean norm (v ⬝ᵥ v); bounds compared in [0, ∞]; reason: same values; no integrability side conditions needed
--   - reading: 𝒰ⁱ_c (Def. 3.1): uⁱ ∈ 𝒰^{i,0}_c and uⁱ is 𝓕^y-adapted, 𝓕^y = ⋁ⱼ 𝓕^{yʲ} (profile left implicit) → a deviation uⁱ is measured against 𝓕^y generated by the observations (3) under the profile (uⁱ, ū⁻ⁱ); ūⁱ against those under ū; reason: 𝓕^y depends on all controls through x⁽ᴺ⁾, u⁽ᴺ⁾ in (3); this is the profile in which uⁱ is played
--   - correction: M^P of (23) (and App. B): ⟨(α₁²Q + D̄ᵀPD̄)x⁰ + 2D̄ᵀPb̄ − 2α₁q, x⁰⟩ + 2α₂β₂⟨Su⁰, x⁰⟩ + b̄ᵀPb̄ + σᵀPσ + σ̄ᵀPσ̄ + ⟨(β₁²R + F̄ᵀPF̄)u⁰ + 2F̄ᵀPb̄ − 2β₁r, u⁰⟩ → the same plus 2⟨D̄ᵀPF̄u⁰, x⁰⟩; reason: Itô's formula for ⟨PXⁱ, Xⁱ⟩ produces the cross term of ⟨P(D̄x⁰ + F̄u⁰ + b̄), D̄x⁰ + F̄u⁰ + b̄⟩; counterexample to (25) as printed: n = m = d = 1, D̄ = F̄ = R = σ̌ = 1, other data 0, P ≡ 1, x⁰ ≡ u⁰ ≡ 1, u = 0, T > 0: J_i(0) = 0 but printed J^P_i(0) + ½⟨P₀x, x⟩ = −T
--   - encoding: σᵀPσ + σ̄ᵀPσ̄ in M^P (23) and M̃^P (p.17), σ, σ̄ ∈ ℝ^{n×d} → tr(σᵀPσ) + tr(σ̄ᵀPσ̄); reason: the Itô correction of ⟨PX, X⟩ is the trace (App. B); for d = 1 the printed expression is this number
--   - correction: p.17: Q̂^P = −β₂S + B̄ᵀP + F̄ᵀPD and S̃^P = F̄ᵀPD̄ + α₂β₂S → Q̂^P = −β₂Sᵀ + B̄ᵀP + F̄ᵀPD and S̃^P = D̄ᵀPF̄ + α₂β₂S; reason: ill-typed for m ≠ n (S ∈ ℝ^{n×m}, Q̂^P ∈ ℝ^{m×n}; F̄ᵀPD̄ ∈ ℝ^{m×n}, S̃^P ∈ ℝ^{n×m}); these are the terms Itô's formula for ⟨Pxⁱ, xⁱ⟩ produces
--   - correction: p.17: R̃^P = β₁R + F̄ᵀPF̄ → R̃^P = β₁²R + F̄ᵀPF̄; reason: the cost (5) contributes β₁²⟨Ru⁽ᴺ⁾, u⁽ᴺ⁾⟩; counterexample to Lemma 5.5 as printed: n = m = d = N = 1, P = 0, R = σ̌ = 1, β₁ = 2, other data 0, u ≡ 1, T > 0: 𝒥_1 = T/2, printed 𝒥^P_1 = −T/2
--   - encoding: p.17: q̄^P = −α₁q + b̄ᵀPD̄ (a row vector) → q̄^P = −α₁q + D̄ᵀPb̄; reason: the same vector written as a column
--   - generalization: P the relaxed compensator of §5 → any P ∈ Υ([0,T]; 𝕊ⁿ), C₀ allowed to depend on P; reason: 𝒥^P_i − J^P_i = 𝒥_i − J_i for every P ∈ Υ (Lemmas 4.7, 5.5), so the bound does not depend on which P; the relaxed compensator is one such P
-- source:
--   Chen, Nie & Wu, Indefinite Linear-Quadratic Partially Observed Mean-Field Game, arXiv:2508.01568v1, Lemma 5.9, p.18

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_Peng1990_SMP_Stochastic
import Definitions.Def_IndefPOMFG_Coeffs
import Definitions.Def_IndefPOMFG_Space
import Definitions.Def_IndefPOMFG_NPlayer
